  Equilibrium comes out of movement. Movement doesn't come out of equilibrium.
  If the whole cosmos where to reach total equilibrium, movement would become unretrievable.
  Everything would remain dead forever.

  Luckily that's impossible.

  Energy produces movement. Energy changes form but it's indestructible.
  So movement is eternal in both temporal directions.

  Matter is made of various particles, among which: atoms.
  The structure of an atom is similar to the one of the solar system: items gravitate around a bigger item.
  In an atom the planets are called electrons.
  The sun is called the nucleus and is an aggregation of protons and neutrons.
  Let's ignore neutrons.

  Electrons repel electrons. Protons repel protons. Electrons and protons are attracted to each other.
  Assuming a one-dimension space (which is a valid assumption if we also assume two particles in isolation),
  this reciprocal attraction could be represented with two vectors of opposite sign.
  The sign of these vectors is called polarity. Which means: electrons and protons have opposite polarities.
  By convention, electrons get the minus sign and protons get the positive sign.
  Neutrons have a neutral polarity (no contribuion to attraction nor repulsion, that's why we're ignoring them).

  The amount of contribution to this attraction / repulsion phenomenon is a property of matter called electric charge.
  A charge with positive polarity is called a positive charge; a charge with negative polarity is called a negative charge.
  (Also:  neutral polarity -> neutral charge.)

  The charge of one proton and the charge of one electron have an equal absolute value called elementary charge. Usual symbol: q.
  From where:
    Equilibrium for an atom means equal number of protons and electrons. (Neutral polarity, no urge to move.)
    The atom is then said stable, otherwise it is said unstable.
  Sometimes a particle carrying an elementary charge (charge carrier) is called a charge.
  So the word charge can have two distinct meanings: a particle, or a property of matter (quantitative or qualitative).
  Beware of context. (Here I'll try to stick with the qualitative meaning: the property of matter.)
  
  An unstable atom has an electric charge. It's called an ion.
  Its protons are at the nucleus, they cannot leave the atom without destroying it.
  So the electric charge has to be due to a lack or an excess of electrons.
  
  A positively charged atom is lacking one or several electrons. It's called a cation.
  A negatively charged atom has one or several extra electrons. It's called an anion.
  Cations attract electrons. Anions repel electrons.

  Let's consider two clusters of ions with different charges somehow isolated from everything, even each other (more on that later).
  Equilibrium requires that the more negative cluster send electrons to the less negative one.
  This results in an urge for electrons to move from the more negative point to the more positive point.
  THIS IS IMPORTANT:
    Both points can have the same polarity (+ & + || - & -) or not (+ & - || - & +), as long as their charge differ, electrons will
    have an urge to move.
    In fact one of the two could even have a neutral charge: if the other is positive it will attract electrons, if it's negative the
    neutral point will attract electrons.
  THIS IS ALSO IMPORTANT:
    In case electrons somehow manage to move, the equilibrium they will eventually reach will be relative.
    Meaning that they will not move until both points have reached a neutral charge: this can very well be impossible.
    They will move until both points have the same charge (which could be positive or negative).
    Indeed if their movement is from the more negative point to the more positive one, equal charge prevents movement.

  As long as our two clusters/points are isolated from each other there is an urge to move but no opportunity to move.
  Aristotle would say there's movement in potency (δύναμις) but not in actuality (ἐντελέχεια).
    //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potentiality_and_actuality
  Modern physics call this phenomenon potential energy.

  (I had a hard time understanding what follows so I suggest you read it slowly.)
  One charge carrier does not store potential energy on its own. It could very well be in a stable atom. And even that would in fact
  be irrelevant.
  Potential energy comes from the proximity of points in matter with different electric charges.
  Which means that the energy in one charge carrier is dependant upon the other charge carriers that surround it.
  So the greater the difference in charge between both points, the greater the energy per charge at each point.
  The energy per charge at one point is called the electric potential (of said point).
  Notice that the electrons' urge to move grows with the electric potential.

  (Coulomb's law adds: it also grows with the proximity of charges. Good to know but we won't be needing it.)

  Now let's assume the urge to move is somehow suddenly satisfied (the charged points are now isolated from everything but each other).
  If electrons can move, they will. Potential energy becomes kinetic energy...
  This sounds very generic. We can go more microscopic than that.

  Electric potential is a scalar (i.e. not a vector). It has no direction. It says how much a particle is urged to move -- not whereto.
  If I say "Socrates is 70 years old", you don't need more information to know Socrates' age. Age is a scalar.
  If I say "Pheidippides ran about 240 km in two days", you might want to know from where to where. Distance is a scalar.
  Sometimes a scalar lacks information, like for instance "from Athens to Sparta".
  Now Pheidippides' performance has a magnitude (about 240 km) and a direction (from Athens to Sparta) -- it's a vector.
  The vector associated with electric potential is called electric field.
  It's called a field because it's actually several vectors across the three dimensions of space, originating from all points which
  contribute by having an electric potential.
  (Their sum is counter-intuitively discrete: remember elementary charge.)
  Addition can be performed on vectors, so the electric field can be considered one vector per point in space.
  Both conceptions peacefully co-exist mathematically.

  Since we're building a universe from scratch and so far only have two points with a difference in electric potential, our electric field
  has one dimension: its direction is from the more negative point to the more positive point -- its magnitude is dependant upon said
  difference in electric potential.


